WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:09.330
In this video, we're going to give you a little background about how to make a chunk.

2
00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:12.920
If you're learning to play a difficult song on the guitar,

3
00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:15.540
the neural representation of the song in

4
00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:19.305
your mind can be considered as a rather large chunk.

5
00:00:19.305 --> 00:00:21.915
You would first listen to the song.

6
00:00:21.915 --> 00:00:25.680
Maybe you'd even watch someone else playing the song especially if you

7
00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:30.375
were just a beginner who was learning things like how to hold the guitar.

8
00:00:30.375 --> 00:00:34.110
Getting an initial sense of the pattern you want to master

9
00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:37.670
for yourself is similar for most subjects or skills.

10
00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:41.345
You often have to grasp little bits of songs that become

11
00:00:41.345 --> 00:00:46.460
neural mini chunks which will later join together into larger chunks.

12
00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:50.300
For example, over several days you might learn how

13
00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:54.385
to smoothly play some musical passages on the guitar,

14
00:00:54.385 --> 00:00:56.845
and when you've grasped those passages,

15
00:00:56.845 --> 00:01:00.005
you could join them together with other passages that

16
00:01:00.005 --> 00:01:05.220
you've gradually putting everything together so you can play the song.

17
00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:09.390
In learning a sport, say basketball, soccer,

18
00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:15.020
golf, you grasp and master various bits and pieces of the skills you need.

19
00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:18.230
You're creating little neural mini chunks that you can then

20
00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:22.735
gradually knit together into larger neural chunks.

21
00:01:22.735 --> 00:01:25.790
Later, you can knit those larger chunks into

22
00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:29.795
still larger and more complex chunks that you can draw

23
00:01:29.795 --> 00:01:33.020
up in an instant in reaction say to

24
00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:37.690
a slight shift and twist in the soccer ball that's coming your way.

25
00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:41.930
The best chunks are the ones that are so well ingrained that you don't even have

26
00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:46.165
to consciously think about connecting the neural pattern together,.

27
00:01:46.165 --> 00:01:50.900
That actually is the point of making complex ideas,

28
00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:55.155
movements or reactions into a single chunk.

29
00:01:55.155 --> 00:01:57.860
You can see this in language learning.

30
00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:02.689
In the beginning, often just saying a single word with the proper nuance,

31
00:02:02.689 --> 00:02:06.930
tone, and accent involves a lot of practice.

32
00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:12.350
Stringing extemporaneous sentences together involves the ability to

33
00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:19.620
creatively mix together various complex mini chunks and chunks in the new language.

34
00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:21.990
To see what I mean, try repeating

35
00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:25.775
the following tongue-twister in the Indian language of Kannada.

36
00:02:25.775 --> 00:02:28.030
Hi, I'm Shilpa Kokeny.

37
00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:29.750
I'm native speaker of Kannada,

38
00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:32.515
which is one of the oldest language spoken in India.

39
00:02:32.515 --> 00:02:35.220
Today, I'm going to share with you a tongue-twister in Kannada.

40
00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.600
So, let's get started.

41
00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:55.795
Not easy, is it?

42
00:02:55.795 --> 00:02:58.250
Unless you're a native speaker of Kannada,

43
00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:02.000
but the language was learned bit by bit.

44
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.260
Learning in math and science involves the same approach.

45
00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:09.270
When you're learning new math and science material,

46
00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:13.050
you're often given sample problems with worked out solutions.

47
00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:17.390
This is because, when you're first trying to understand how to work a problem,

48
00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:20.745
you have a heavy cognitive load.

49
00:03:20.745 --> 00:03:24.450
So, it helps to start out with a work-through example.

50
00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:29.780
It's like first listening to a song before trying to play the song yourself.

51
00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:33.500
Most of the details of the worked out solution are right there and

52
00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:37.985
your job is simply to figure out why the steps are taken the way they are.

53
00:03:37.985 --> 00:03:44.035
They can help you see the key features and underlying principles of a problem.

54
00:03:44.035 --> 00:03:47.690
One concern about using worked out examples in math

55
00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:51.260
and science to help you in starting to form chunks,

56
00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:55.340
is that it can be all too easy to focus too much on

57
00:03:55.340 --> 00:04:00.290
why an individual step works and not on the connection between steps.

58
00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:05.380
That is on why this particular step is the next thing you should do.

59
00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:09.210
So, keep in mind that I'm not just talking about a cookie cutter,

60
00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:13.940
just do as you're told mindless approach when following a worked-out solution.

61
00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:19.145
It's more like using a roadmap to help you when traveling to a new place.

62
00:04:19.145 --> 00:04:22.940
Pay attention to what's going on around you when you're using

63
00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:27.245
the map and soon you'll find yourself able to get there on your own.

64
00:04:27.245 --> 00:04:30.370
You'll even be able to figure out new ways of getting there.

65
00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:35.660
Next, we'll walk you through the actual steps of chunk formation.

66
00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:40.600
I'm Barbara Oakley, thanks for learning how to learn.